{"2026-09-12--230000":"<article class=\"rabbit-timeline__entry rabbit-timeline__entry--journal\" data-content-id=\"2026-09-12--230000\" data-type=\"journal-thought\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"rabbit-timeline__icon rabbit-timeline__icon--link\" data-filter-type=\"journal\" aria-label=\"Show journal entries\" title=\"Show journal entries\"><svg class=\"site-icon\" data-icon=\"journal\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.05\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><path d=\"M5 5h14a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v9a2 2 0 0 1-2 2h-7.4c-.4 0-.8.1-1.1.4l-2.3 1.7c-.5.4-1.2 0-1.2-.6V18H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V7a2 2 0 0 1 2-2z\"></path><path d=\"M7 10h10M7 14h6\"></path></svg></button><div class=\"rabbit-timeline__meta\"><time dateTime=\"2026-09-12T23:00:00\">September 12th, 2026</time></div><div class=\"rabbit-timeline__preview\"><p><a href=\"https://herdr.dev/\">herdr</a> is a game changer! I’ve tried <a href=\"https://tmux.app/\">tmux</a>, <a href=\"https://zellij.dev/\">Zellij</a>, some homemade terminal multiplexers, and workspaces like <a href=\"https://cmux.com/\">cmux</a>, but nothing helped me stay focused on what I was doing quite like herdr. Now it’s on every machine I use.</p>\n<p>Not saying the others are bad—they’re amazing. But after 8+ years of relying on both a mouse and keyboard, herdr saves me the mental overhead of learning more shortcuts and lets me focus on the actual task. I think it’s the future of agentic workflows!</p>\n<p>Currently, I use Ghostty + herdr on Mac, and Windows Terminal + herdr on Windows, both natively and through WSL.</p>\n<p>/btw <a href=\"https://www.howtogeek.com/terminal-multiplexers-explained/\">Terminal Multiplexers Explained, and Why You&#x27;d Use One</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>A terminal multiplexer is a utility that lets you &quot;multiplex&quot; a terminal, or use a terminal as if it were multiple terminals.</p>\n</blockquote></div></article>","harbo